---
/* The programme: every evening, one screen each, in the order of the site.
 *
 * The full-screen scroll-snap format is the client's requirement and not a
 * design choice to renegotiate — CLAUDE.md rule 1 — so its costs are paid
 * inside the constraint: every evening is also a page of its own from PR 9,
 * `prefers-reduced-motion` turns this into an ordinary list, and the titles are
 * `<h2>` under one page-level `<h1>` instead of eighty-one `<h1>`s.
 *
 * The scroller opens on the first evening still to come, which is the one thing
 * here that no CSS can do: a document opens at the top, and the position of a
 * scrolling container is not something a stylesheet sets. Hence the inline
 * script at the foot of this file — ten lines, no dependency, and a plain
 * degradation if it does not run.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Modal from '../components/Modal.astro';
import Scene from '../components/Scene.astro';
import { loadProgramme } from '../lib/programme.ts';

const { scenes, nextIndex } = await loadProgramme();
---

<Base title="Il programma — La Miniera Culturale in Periferia">
  {/* The design has no room for a title of the page: the evenings carry the
      headings. It is said rather than shown — for a screen reader arriving at
      the top, and for a crawler reading a page whose <h2>s would otherwise
      hang off nothing. */}
  <h1 class="visually-hidden">Il programma della Miniera Culturale in Periferia</h1>

  <div class="scroller" data-scroller>
    {
      scenes.map((scene, index) => (
        <Scene scene={scene} open={index === nextIndex} eager={index === nextIndex} />
      ))
    }
  </div>

  {/* What the «Prenota il posto» button shows, written once for the whole
      programme: it is the same for every evening still to come, so it does not
      belong inside a scene. In a <template> because it has no place in the page
      until it is asked for — unlike the links of an evening, which are content
      and stay in the markup.

      The number to write to is not here yet: it is the president's, the design
      only ever had a placeholder, and publishing a wrong one is worse than
      publishing none. PR 10 puts it in, and with it the direct link that gives
      this button something to do with scripting off. */}
  <template id="booking">
    <p>
      La sala ha sessanta posti. Per tenerne uno si scrive all'organizzatore con
      il proprio nome e quante persone siete: la risposta arriva in serata.
    </p>
    <p>
      Se non si prenota si può venire lo stesso, ma il posto non è garantito.
    </p>
  </template>

  <Modal />
</Base>

{/* Inline and synchronous, right after the markup it moves: it runs before the
    first paint, so the programme is *drawn* at the right evening instead of
    being drawn at the top and jumping. `is:inline` keeps Astro from bundling it
    into a deferred module, which would put the jump after the paint.

    Measured rather than computed from an index: with `content-visibility` the
    heights are the declared intrinsic ones, and asking the element where it is
    gets the same answer without this file knowing how tall a scene is.

    Without JavaScript nothing here happens: the programme opens on the oldest
    evening and scrolls normally. That is the whole degradation. */}
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var scroller = document.querySelector('[data-scroller]');
    var open = scroller && scroller.querySelector('[data-open]');
    if (!open) return;
    scroller.scrollTop +=
      open.getBoundingClientRect().top - scroller.getBoundingClientRect().top;
  })();
</script>

<style>
  /* The scroller is the page: the document itself does not scroll, this does.
     One screen per evening, snapping to the top of each.

     `--scene-height` is `svh` with a `vh` fallback declared in @supports — with
     `dvh` the height changes as Safari's address bar retracts and, under
     mandatory snap, the position jumps (rule 5). */
  .scroller {
    height: var(--scene-height);
    overflow-y: auto;
    scroll-snap-type: y mandatory;

    /* Hidden, as in the design: the Timeline of PR 8 is what tells a reader
       where they are, and a scrollbar over 81 evenings says almost nothing. */
    scrollbar-width: none;
  }

  .scroller::-webkit-scrollbar {
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
  }
</style>
